in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Flax, also known as common flax or line, is a flowering plant, Linum usitatissimum, in the family Linaceae. It is cultivated as a food and fiber crop in regions of the world with temperate climates."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-flaxseed"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flaxseed"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Nepalese cuisine","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine",[["Ghonghi","dish/ghonghi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Freshwater snail","ingredient/freshwater-snail"]]}]},"ingredient/cooking-banana":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cooking-banana","n":"Cooking banana","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Cooking bananas are a group of banana cultivars in the genus Musa whose fruits are generally used in cooking. They are not eaten raw and are generally starchy. Many cooking bananas are referred to as plantains or green bananas. In botanical usage, the term plantain is used only for true plantains, while other starchy cultivars used for cooking are called cooking bananas. True plantains are cooking cultivars belonging to the AAB group, while cooking bananas are any cooking cultivar belonging to the AAB, AAA, ABB, or BBB groups. The currently accepted scientific name for all such cultivars in these groups is Musa × paradisiaca. Fe'i bananas (Musa × troglodytarum) from the Pacific Islands are often eaten roasted or boiled, and are thus informally referred to as mountain plantains, although they do not belong to any of the species from which all modern banana cultivars are descended."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cooking-banana"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooking_banana"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Jibarito","dish/jibarito"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Nagasari","dish/nagasari"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Lettuce","ingredient/lettuce"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Plantain (cooking)","ingredient/plantain-cooking"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Pandanus","ingredient/pandanus"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/86/Mega_racimos_de_guineos.jpg/500px-Mega_racimos_de_guineos.jpg","w":500,"h":750,"by":"J & E","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mega_racimos_de_guineos.jpg"}},"dish/peach-melba":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/peach-melba","n":"Peach Melba","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Peach Melba is a dessert of peaches and raspberry sauce with vanilla ice cream. It was invented in 1892 or 1893 by the French chef Auguste Escoffier at the Savoy Hotel, London, to honour the Australian soprano Nellie Melba."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-peach-melba"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_Melba"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Ice cream","ingredient/ice-cream"],["Peach","ingredient/peach"],["Raspberry","ingredient/raspberry"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Vanilla ice cream","ingredient/vanilla-ice-cream"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pa